WEBVTT

NOTE Narrated by a synthetic voice (an ElevenLabs stock voice); the opening disclosure is the macOS Trinoids voice. Text by Phil Renato.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.246
This audiobook is narrated by a synthetic voice.

00:00:04.446 --> 00:00:05.375
Crafting Error

00:00:06.375 --> 00:00:07.768
Phil Renato.

00:00:10.768 --> 00:00:13.276
It’s been nearly fourteen billion years,

00:00:13.276 --> 00:00:16.527
since particles and antiparticles started pairing off,

00:00:16.527 --> 00:00:22.796
tumbling, colliding; losing one another in a vast, nucleosynthetic field.

00:00:22.796 --> 00:00:26.279
We are fibers of crosslinked carbon, twined together,

00:00:26.279 --> 00:00:28.090
born from waveless water,

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:31.248
stretching, tearing, emerging patterns,

00:00:31.248 --> 00:00:35.242
condensing at the interface between fermions and stars.

00:00:35.242 --> 00:00:37.332
I am not a witness, nor the subject,

00:00:37.332 --> 00:00:41.744
just an accidental target for sputtering bits of vaporized information,

00:00:41.744 --> 00:00:45.970
Billions bounce off, a few carve in, the rest pass through,

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:49.685
I’m a consumer—suckling air while learning to breathe milk,

00:00:49.685 --> 00:00:53.261
slowly learning to tell blood from fire.

00:00:53.261 --> 00:00:56.465
You are smoke, precipitating match heads,

00:00:56.465 --> 00:01:00.227
wetting cardboard pages, stapling them into tiny books,

00:01:00.227 --> 00:01:04.685
drowning words, dissolving them in wine, flooding libraries,

00:01:04.685 --> 00:01:08.400
emitting stellar clouds that cross sense and time.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.765
Sources are light bringers, writhing and scratching against one another,

00:01:12.765 --> 00:01:16.852
sparking, wearing down, so bright they burn.

00:01:19.852 --> 00:01:25.733
Crafting Error is about creation. What do I look at? How do I make sense of these

00:01:25.733 --> 00:01:31.394
things? How do the tensions between my various influences bend my objects away

00:01:31.394 --> 00:01:36.614
from what they may have been if I consumed other things? These words are

00:01:36.614 --> 00:01:42.201
about source material, specifically my own. These paragraphs are observations

00:01:42.201 --> 00:01:48.156
about the things I read, look at and the forces they exert on the things I make in

00:01:48.156 --> 00:01:53.449
my studio. These experimental essays, aphorisms and poems work through my

00:01:53.449 --> 00:01:59.404
objects, ideas and identity. I speculate about why things attract my attention and

00:01:59.404 --> 00:02:02.345
how and why they relate to designcraft.

00:02:02.345 --> 00:02:09.279
“The world is given but once. Nothing is reflected. The original and the mirror

00:02:09.279 --> 00:02:14.599
image are identical. The world extended in space and time is but our

00:02:14.599 --> 00:02:15.949
representation.”.

00:02:15.949 --> 00:02:21.967
Footnote 1. Erwin Schrodinger, Mind and Matter, Cambridge University Press, 1944,

00:02:21.967 --> 00:02:27.986
from What is Life? With Mind and Matter and Autobiographical Sketches, 1967, page

00:02:27.986 --> 00:02:33.252
136. ES was echoing and extending Schopenhauer’s “The world extended in

00:02:33.252 --> 00:02:36.336
space and time is but our representation”.

00:02:36.336 --> 00:02:41.287
I’m a visual artist. Specifically, I design and make things that are jewelry-

00:02:41.287 --> 00:02:46.174
and product-adjacent. I’ve tried to generalize the topics of this book to be

00:02:46.174 --> 00:02:50.669
of some value to anyone who takes the time to read it. But the primary

00:02:50.669 --> 00:02:55.295
chemistry is the recursive reaction between the particular sources mixed

00:02:55.295 --> 00:03:00.377
together in my studio beakers. They’re different from yours, and I’m looking at

00:03:00.377 --> 00:03:03.178
what comes out of them in Phil-centric ways.

00:03:03.178 --> 00:03:07.944
In trying to explain what I’ve been writing over the past year, I’ve been

00:03:07.944 --> 00:03:12.711
tempted to call it a work on design theory. But that’s too broad, while I

00:03:12.711 --> 00:03:17.941
engage in some theory here, it isn’t a general theory of design. It isn’t even a

00:03:17.941 --> 00:03:23.038
special theory of design, or a single narrative idea. The chapters in Crafting

00:03:23.038 --> 00:03:27.871
Error are the product of one person typing in the late two thousand teens,

00:03:27.871 --> 00:03:33.233
and were written consecutively, but they draw from decades of collecting, reading,

00:03:33.233 --> 00:03:38.198
and thinking about art, design, craft, culture, the individual, physics, and

00:03:38.198 --> 00:03:43.296
even a little metaphysics. There are fairly big swings of style and substance.

00:03:43.296 --> 00:03:48.526
The two consistent elements are pairings of the image and the word—which doesn’t

00:03:48.526 --> 00:03:53.557
really separate this work from any other effort at explaining art and design.

00:03:53.557 --> 00:03:58.853
Sometimes my verbs correspond directly to my image, but other times the links are

00:03:58.853 --> 00:04:03.818
pretty loose; maybe even broken. You won’t find these things neatly unpacked

00:04:03.818 --> 00:04:09.048
and examined in a way you can directly apply to your own work—partly because I’m

00:04:09.048 --> 00:04:14.211
not sure I want to do that, and partly because I’m not sure I can do that. It’s

00:04:14.211 --> 00:04:19.309
an anticonfluential approach; not a highly portable theory. This is a journal

00:04:19.309 --> 00:04:24.340
about my things and thing-making, about atoms and forms and symbols as I have

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:26.260
measured them. It gets messy.

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:32.005
Crafting Error was made possible by a sabbatical granted by Kendall College of

00:04:32.005 --> 00:04:37.526
Art and Design, where I teach jewelry, as well as industrial design. It was

00:04:37.526 --> 00:04:43.643
primarily written during the fall of 2015 and owes its existence to the patient and

00:04:43.643 --> 00:04:49.537
generous mentors, colleagues, friends, family, and students who have indulged me

00:04:49.537 --> 00:04:51.477
over the past few decades.

00:04:51.477 --> 00:04:57.397
Skip Hunter, Bob Chew, John Marshall, Dan Loewenstein, and Mary Lee Hu were exactly

00:04:57.397 --> 00:05:03.172
the professors I needed. They taught me some of the things I needed to know about

00:05:03.172 --> 00:05:08.587
my field; but, more importantly, they helped me develop a critical posture I

00:05:08.587 --> 00:05:14.073
could use to see how my work might relate to the world outside my discipline.

00:05:14.073 --> 00:05:19.415
Consistently, I was pushed to test my ideas against others; to discriminate

00:05:19.415 --> 00:05:25.263
between various options, to slow down and consider the consequences of my actions.

00:05:25.263 --> 00:05:30.533
(Then I sped up to collect more junk—I am still working on this project.).

00:05:30.533 --> 00:05:35.706
Pamela Argentieri, Boris Bally, Doug Bucci, Tom Edwards, Susanna Engbers,

00:05:35.706 --> 00:05:41.454
Teresa Faris, Mike Gayk, Matthew Hollern, Stanley Lechtzin, Adam Schuitema, Brent

00:05:41.454 --> 00:05:46.986
Skidmore, Heath Satow, Courtney Starrett, Rebecca Strzelec, Annie Wassman, Don

00:05:46.986 --> 00:05:52.159
Freidlich, and other peers, colleagues, and mentors helped me to keep the

00:05:52.159 --> 00:05:57.835
important things in focus while keeping my temper from overexposing the scene. I

00:05:57.835 --> 00:06:03.512
have misanthropic tendencies, but the few truly exceptional people I’ve met have

00:06:03.512 --> 00:06:09.259
kept me from becoming entirely estranged from the community of man. They read and

00:06:09.259 --> 00:06:14.935
react to my dumb ideas, give me feedback on my stream of ill-considered objects,

00:06:14.935 --> 00:06:20.827
and inspire me to work a little harder, a little smarter, and to be a little nicer.

00:06:20.827 --> 00:06:24.635
I am lucky to have smart friends who make time for me.

00:06:24.635 --> 00:06:30.186
I think of myself as a designer, but the truth is that I am a teacher. That is a

00:06:30.186 --> 00:06:35.526
professional distinction—as I have made far more money and have had many more

00:06:35.526 --> 00:06:40.866
clients as a professor than as a graphic or jewelry designer. But it’s also a

00:06:40.866 --> 00:06:46.277
point of view; to the extent that I am still an artist it is because this work

00:06:46.277 --> 00:06:50.704
keeps me thinking and learning. This monologue is my aura in the

00:06:50.704 --> 00:06:55.763
classroom—it’s what I draw on as an instructor and as a mentor (and it is

00:06:55.763 --> 00:07:01.243
ongoing.) We could think about students as raw materials, work products, agents

00:07:01.243 --> 00:07:06.584
and the creators of the knowledge they acquire; and all of these perspectives

00:07:06.584 --> 00:07:12.275
are correct in at least some situations. Facilitating these requires a designer, a

00:07:12.275 --> 00:07:17.475
maker, a critic and a reader—I help craft students. And that work, in turn,

00:07:17.475 --> 00:07:18.318
reshapes me.

00:07:18.318 --> 00:07:23.598
I’ve led a privileged life, which would not have been possible without the

00:07:23.598 --> 00:07:28.589
support and encouragement of my family—especially my mother, Marietta.

00:07:28.589 --> 00:07:34.158
Although I am partially her fault, it takes a tough, generous, wise, tolerant,

00:07:34.158 --> 00:07:39.438
farsighted, crazy, or just persistent woman to have supported me during my

00:07:39.438 --> 00:07:44.935
adolescence and early adulthood. It has taken an altogether different kind of

00:07:44.935 --> 00:07:50.576
person to have put up with me in my fully formed condition. My wife, Elizabeth,

00:07:50.576 --> 00:07:56.001
spends much of her time feeding and cleaning up after a herd of goats, dogs,

00:07:56.001 --> 00:08:01.425
cats, and a horse; sweet, strong, but hopelessly unruly creatures. While I’m

00:08:01.425 --> 00:08:07.067
more or less housebroken, I am messier, less obedient, and far less cuddly than

00:08:07.067 --> 00:08:12.202
Saul, Mocha, Maggie, Mila, Pablo, Luna, Betty, Bob, Memo, Angel, Cosimo,

00:08:12.202 --> 00:08:17.193
Penelope, and Picasso. Elizabeth puts up with my fleet of Pontiacs, my

00:08:17.193 --> 00:08:23.051
heterochromic shoes and year-round shorts wearing, my pathological narcissism, and

00:08:23.051 --> 00:08:28.476
my reticence to engage with human beings that I don’t have to interact with.

00:08:28.476 --> 00:08:30.718
Elizabeth is my loving enabler.

00:08:30.718 --> 00:08:36.107
My instinct is to dedicate this work to my brother. But since he has been dead

00:08:36.107 --> 00:08:41.425
for twenty-three years, there is no Brian to read or try to make sense of the

00:08:41.425 --> 00:08:46.464
inscription. He exists in the dry chemistry of photographs, memories, and

00:08:46.464 --> 00:08:51.783
decisions. He catalyzed every image before 1993, whether resting in albums or

00:08:51.783 --> 00:08:57.451
constructed in my mind. And he has been a reagent in every choice I’ve made since.

00:08:57.451 --> 00:09:02.980
I don’t so much ask what he would have done in my place, as try to use what time

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:08.438
I have to synthesize something that will outlast me; things that may provoke in

00:09:08.438 --> 00:09:13.407
others the longing I feel for his presence. So I dedicate the matter and

00:09:13.407 --> 00:09:18.796
meaning in this book to me; or to the parts of me that still remember him, the

00:09:18.796 --> 00:09:24.045
parts of me still capable of change — the fair, funny, and sweet parts of my

00:09:24.045 --> 00:09:26.214
solution that buffer the acrid.

00:09:26.214 --> 00:09:33.337
Sporadic, recursive reactions to objects, forms, purposes, intent, effects,

00:09:33.337 --> 00:09:36.802
objectification, and their people...

00:09:36.802 --> 00:09:42.583
Modeling clay is a stage of psychosexual development unto itself, squeezed between

00:09:42.583 --> 00:09:46.723
the anal and the phallic. These wet mounds are terrifyingly

00:09:46.723 --> 00:09:52.504
shapeless—amorphous metamatter in danger of suffocating the world. It’s no wonder

00:09:52.504 --> 00:09:58.357
that the formulas we give to children all crack, dry and fall away; their oxidation

00:09:58.357 --> 00:10:03.924
protects us from having to live with the consequences of creation. We give kids

00:10:03.924 --> 00:10:09.348
extruders and presses and kits to mimic burgers and cakes and candies so that

00:10:09.348 --> 00:10:14.487
they can get a head start on their consumption and careers. Beating salty

00:10:14.487 --> 00:10:20.126
cellulose into molds drowns a weapon that geniuses and morons might have used to

00:10:20.126 --> 00:10:25.693
threaten the species. The paper plans that come with these toys protect us—they

00:10:25.693 --> 00:10:31.403
keep us from building too many new worlds on the old, from seeing each new cup of

00:10:31.403 --> 00:10:34.900
clay as a chance to plunge the aged into darkness.

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:40.418
The average orifice isn’t terribly busy; it’s a pair of lungs waiting for a probe

00:10:40.418 --> 00:10:45.936
or a pipe to poke in. It’s an exchange, a semi-recognizable interface for fire to

00:10:45.936 --> 00:10:51.177
move through its occasionally closed circuit. The opening is usually gated or

00:10:51.177 --> 00:10:56.557
shielded, plugged up with something or kept closed until coupling. But we still

00:10:56.557 --> 00:11:01.868
need to know where to put things in, so the inlets have evolved to stay puffed

00:11:01.868 --> 00:11:07.386
and prone. They invite use with arrows, lights and lipstick—marking entrances and

00:11:07.386 --> 00:11:09.869
exits. Some work harder than others:

00:11:09.869 --> 00:11:15.102
The sparkplug fills the otherwise open head with light and leaves its fragile

00:11:15.102 --> 00:11:20.060
tip poking out of the cavity. The manifold butts up to exhaust ports that

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:25.569
carry spent gases to a platinum hive. The lips part to take in oxygen and express

00:11:25.569 --> 00:11:30.664
carbon dioxide with each cycle, sucking in and pushing out something of the

00:11:30.664 --> 00:11:33.832
other. They expand, are satisfied and contract.

00:11:33.832 --> 00:11:39.015
The mirror, like the camera, has flipped from object to idea—from optical

00:11:39.015 --> 00:11:44.559
instrument to a backward self-image. But the mirror was water, bronze and gold

00:11:44.559 --> 00:11:50.030
before it was metalized glass. These quicksilver discs are flatter and whiter

00:11:50.030 --> 00:11:55.574
than ever, but that isn’t you in your reflection. Narcissus too was tricked by

00:11:55.574 --> 00:12:01.261
the distortions in these tiny backwards worlds. It should be enough to know that

00:12:01.261 --> 00:12:06.804
what you see in the mirror is not what others see. The cleaner the mirror, the

00:12:06.804 --> 00:12:11.988
better you see, the less you know. The worst crime in our history was not

00:12:11.988 --> 00:12:17.603
putting the material gold onto fingers, the felony was its polished refinement:

00:12:17.603 --> 00:12:22.571
our reflection. This flat technology makes the schizophrenic tick look

00:12:22.571 --> 00:12:27.610
natural, showing images that don’t exist to people trained to see them.

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:33.064
What we lack in chronodynamics we make up for in bias — we are the site, source,

00:12:33.064 --> 00:12:37.836
arbiter and consumer of our own reminiscences. I saw my first immersive

00:12:37.836 --> 00:12:43.290
panorama in 1995, and it seemed like its children would one day take the place of

00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:48.266
memory. Why bother paying attention to something in the round when we will

00:12:48.266 --> 00:12:53.379
eventually have high resolution meshes and images to reconstruct every time,

00:12:53.379 --> 00:12:58.560
space and angle with zoomable precision on demand? With fast enough processor

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:03.401
bandwidth and RAM we may one day be able to trace every molecule in four

00:13:03.401 --> 00:13:08.650
dimensions in fixed bits, through skin and into bones, with algorithms to make

00:13:08.650 --> 00:13:14.104
sense of them for us. Whose face is that? How would you have felt about what they

00:13:14.104 --> 00:13:19.626
just did? Our copper traces will give way to quarks and our fiberglass boards will

00:13:19.626 --> 00:13:24.670
eventually be replaced by circuits on aether. We may be able to turn causes

00:13:24.670 --> 00:13:29.920
into effects using subroutines, but these gated mathematics will never replace

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:30.397
memory.

00:13:30.397 --> 00:13:36.113
The nipple is one of the few forms that evolution has prepared us to recognize

00:13:36.113 --> 00:13:41.457
and love. Sucking is our primary technology; a pathway for life, but also

00:13:41.457 --> 00:13:46.727
a locus of shame. It is a soft, demi-pointed reminder of our dependence.

00:13:46.727 --> 00:13:52.591
Air comes with each suspended milliliter of protein and carbohydrate we consume.

00:13:52.591 --> 00:13:58.381
This fuel oxidizes our blood, collapsing cells, stealing our mass for flames we

00:13:58.381 --> 00:14:04.245
can barely see. Even our minerals break us down. The nipple is mother feeding us

00:14:04.245 --> 00:14:10.332
rich poison. We are born suckling, moving from skin to bottle. We grow into adults,

00:14:10.332 --> 00:14:15.899
gnashing our teeth on flesh. The fetish is the fetal turned carnal, the need

00:14:15.899 --> 00:14:16.790
turned want.

00:14:16.790 --> 00:14:22.137
We don’t want power or speed or variety, we want portals to dimensions where our

00:14:22.137 --> 00:14:27.078
fantasies are default settings. We want the magic that Newton promised us;

00:14:27.078 --> 00:14:32.425
alchemistries so simple that any child could transform words into bananas, their

00:14:32.425 --> 00:14:37.908
yellow into gold and that reflection into everlasting bliss. But we don’t want the

00:14:37.908 --> 00:14:43.323
heat. We want the wormhole, but not the motion sickness, the rhetoric without the

00:14:43.323 --> 00:14:48.534
rebound, the muscles not the iron plates. At the end of every cable is a power

00:14:48.534 --> 00:14:53.611
source, a hub and a translator. This stack identifies, amplifies and resends

00:14:53.611 --> 00:14:58.890
each packet with a note that says I was here. When it arrives, at the other end

00:14:58.890 --> 00:15:03.560
of a billion glass strands or a light year of copper wire, the data is

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:08.637
extracted by separating it from all this noise. But the message, having been

00:15:08.637 --> 00:15:13.307
thickened, is never quite the same; the sound remains in deep whisper.

00:15:13.307 --> 00:15:18.237
Television has warmed a bit. Where we once settled into frozen glass, heating

00:15:18.237 --> 00:15:22.843
the cathode ray tubes with our imaginations, HD pixels now flicker small

00:15:22.843 --> 00:15:27.903
and bright and frequently enough to stand in for all but the best theaters. Our

00:15:27.903 --> 00:15:33.028
living rooms are better than the cinemas of our parents. Channels you can’t name

00:15:33.028 --> 00:15:38.153
have hotter shows than any but the most engrossing from the last century. As the

00:15:38.153 --> 00:15:43.083
box has warmed, we have cooled. Lo-res and paper poor sound were part of what

00:15:43.083 --> 00:15:48.273
kept us watching. As the explosions and lasers have improved they’ve packed frost

00:15:48.273 --> 00:15:53.138
into us. We haven’t yet tuned out, but only because they’re turning more on.

00:15:53.138 --> 00:15:58.198
That movement generates a friction, and before anyone can panic we will stumble

00:15:58.198 --> 00:15:59.236
back into print.
